# The flake ledger: failures this workspace has already seen, ruled on, and
# does not want to meet again quietly.
#
# **What it is for.** A ledgered failure that recurs is the one CI event that
# reads as noise to whoever is looking at it — the reflex is a rerun, and a
# rerun leaves nothing behind. So every entry here carries the ruling that was
# made about it, and `.github/scripts/flake-tripwire.sh` matches a failing
# suite's output against these signatures and writes the recurrence into the
# run: an annotation and a block in the job summary, naming the entry and what
# was decided. Nothing here retries anything and nothing here turns a red run
# green. What it removes is the quiet rerun.
#
# **A match is a second occurrence by construction.** An entry exists because
# the failure already happened at least once and was ruled on; `first-seen`
# says when. So the tripwire has no counting to do — matching is the event.
#
# **Format.** One entry per block, `key: value` per line, blocks separated by a
# blank line. Lines starting with `#` are comments. Five keys, all required:
#
#   id           a stable slug, used in the annotation title
#   signature    a fixed substring matched against the failing suite's output.
#                A test name where the class is one test; the assertion's own
#                text where the class is one condition across several.
#   class        which of the ledger's classes this belongs to
#   first-seen   the date of the occurrence the entry was opened for
#   disposition  the ruling: what was decided, and what a recurrence means now
#
# **What a signature is matched against** is every line of the run except the
# status lines libtest prints for a test that passed or was skipped. That is
# what makes a test name a usable signature: the name is in the output of every
# run of the suite that holds it, and only a failing run puts it on a `...
# FAILED` line, in the `failures:` list, or in panic text. An entry whose class
# is one test therefore names the test, and stays honest through a restructure
# that moves which of the test's assertions trips first; an entry whose class is
# one condition spread across several tests names the condition's own words.
#
# **Removing an entry** is saying the class can no longer recur — the condition
# was removed rather than judged. An entry whose disposition has been overtaken
# is edited rather than deleted, so the recurrence still surfaces.

id: class-a-recovery-handoff
signature: recovery_handoff_reconciles_an_event_observed_during_the_heal
class: wall-clock starvation
first-seen: 2026-08-09
disposition: Ruled 2026-08-17. The 15s work bound stands and is not widened or load-scaled; the host-health preflight gates the evidence instead, so a starved host is a non-qualifying evidence source. A recurrence in CI is read by the bound it breached, as ruled 2026-09-21 under class-a-probe-bound: a probe-bound breach is a runner that starved a probe, non-qualifying on its own timing, and its rerun is deliberate and recorded, not quiet; a work-bound breach with no probe-bound breach behind it still reopens that ruling.

