id: class-a-probe-bound
signature: stopped at probe
class: wall-clock starvation
first-seen: 2026-09-21
disposition: Opened 2026-09-21 off a hosted runner (run 35568591459), where the real-watcher lease acquisition in `crates/norn-testkit/src/isolation.rs` stopped at probe 85, which took 317.834376ms and passed its 250ms probe bound 4.288784299s into a 900s work bound. It recurred 2026-09-28 (PR #238, run 36377364917, in `a_walk_that_refuses_leaves_the_findings_in_its_scope_standing`) at probe 78, which took 255.4ms against the same 250ms bound 3.88s into the same 900s. Fixed for lease acquisition 2026-10-16: acquisition_budget gives its non-blocking lock attempt and holder-record read a fixed 10s probe allowance, independent of the holder's own probe bound. The work wall and per-holder patience are unchanged. A recurrence in lease acquisition now means a probe exceeded that generous allowance and needs diagnosis. The general ruling for other waits remains: the probe bound is not load-scaled and the work bound is not widened. Each of those probes is one non-blocking try-lock, so a probe that took 318ms is a reading of the runner rather than of the product: the process was not scheduled. The class-A disposition of 2026-08-17 extends to hosted runners on that reading: a runner that starves a probe is a non-qualifying evidence source for that run, and the evidence is the probe's own rendering of its duration and bound. The case that fails differs per run, so the signature is the phrase the wait module renders for a probe-bound breach and never for a work-bound one. A recurrence is non-qualifying on its own timing, and its rerun is deliberate and recorded here, not quiet. A work-bound breach with no probe-bound breach behind it is not this entry, and under a class-A entry it reopens that ruling. A run that holds a work-bound breach beside a starved probe is not cleared by this entry either: the work-bound breach is its own reading, so that run is not non-qualifying and its rerun does not clear it.

