id: class-a-lease-changing-hands
signature: a_queue_that_keeps_changing_hands_outlives_the_patience_bound
class: wall-clock starvation
first-seen: 2026-09-14
disposition: Opened 2026-09-14 off the hosted `build and test` job (ubuntu-latest, run 34866765125, job 104052422851): the case's writer handed the record over 269 times, and the waiter still read one holder as unchanged for 503.726504ms against a 500ms patience, so the wait ended at the patience instead of the 2s wall. It recurred 2026-09-21 in CI (PR #176, run 35668830907): 266 hand-overs, 501.1ms against the same 500ms patience, 1.92s into the 2s wall. Ruled with the class-A entries above: the bound is not widened and the case is not load-scaled, because a patience that absorbs a starved runner no longer states the re-arm. Both times the case's own guard, the writer's widest measured gap under the patience, passed, so the recurrence reopened the ruling and made the observation-window arithmetic the subject. Settled 2026-09-28: each measurement could be skewed by a descheduled thread, in opposite directions. The waiter read the clock after reading the record, so a look descheduled between the two charged that slice to the holder; the writer timestamped each gap after its write returned, so a writer descheduled between a write landing and its timestamp understated the gap. Both are now conservative. The waiter's unmoved span is a lower bound, taken between two reads that both saw one record, and a holder that really stands still is still named at the patience, at most one look later. The case's guard bounds an upper bound, the window from before the write that made a record to after the next write that landed, or to the failure for the last one. The unmoved span sits inside that window, so a passing guard leaves the patience no standing to fire on, short of a torn read at a digit carry that matches a later record, which the waiter's gap between looks bounds. Ruled 2026-09-28: a run that fails the case's own guard, its writer stalled past the patience, is a reading of a starved runner, as a probe-bound breach is: non-qualifying on its own timing, and its rerun is deliberate and recorded, not quiet. A recurrence that ends on the patience with the guard passing is a defect in the re-arm or in this arithmetic, not a starved runner, and it is fixed rather than rerun. The case asserts the wait ended on its wall rather than on a probe, so a probe overrun fails it and renders its probe-bound breach; that recurrence is read as ruled 2026-09-21 under class-a-probe-bound: a runner that starved a probe, non-qualifying on its own timing, and its rerun is deliberate and recorded, not quiet.

id: class-c-case-only-parent-rename
signature: a_case_renamed_parent_over_a_save_converges_on_a_build_from_zero
class: case-insensitive volume, case-only rename
first-seen: 2026-09-14
disposition: Opened 2026-09-14 off the per-PR `determinism and filesystem (macOS ARM64)` job (run 34874409655, job 104077923890, PR #161 at 75abd09), on a case-insensitive volume the suite reported as such. The store held `churn/renaming/held.md` where the directory renders `RENAMING`. Fixed 2026-09-27: both routes to that store are closed, and neither by a wider settle. The watcher increment reads and lands a reported path at the spelling each directory lists, so a save's report applied after its directory's rename no longer re-spells the row back, whichever order the two reports are applied in. The churn settle compares the spelling a row is held at with the one the tree renders, so the case no longer reads the store after the save's increment and before the directory's. A recurrence is a defect rather than a flake.

id: maintainer-holder-never-answered
signature: a_killed_maintainer_releases_its_lock
class: harness child that never answered
first-seen: 2026-09-28
disposition: Opened 2026-09-28 off the hosted `build and test` job (ubuntu-latest, run 36369341854, job 108762128920, PR #235), where the holding child wrote no answer inside the parent's 30s work bound and the case discarded the child's run report, so the cause was not recovered. The leading candidate, unproven for this occurrence, is the installed copy's exec failing with ETXTBSY while a sibling case's spawn still carries the copy's write descriptor; that condition is removed under harness-exec-text-file-busy, where `Sandbox::install_binary` copies through a `cp` child. Ruled 2026-09-28: the wait is not widened and the case is not retried; every failed wait on the holding child now names how the child's run ended. A recurrence is read by that report and not cleared by a rerun: `could not be run: Text file busy` is a defect in that ruling, not a flake, and harness-exec-text-file-busy matches it too; `ended with TimedOut` with the child's output is a child wedged before it answered; any other end is a child that died early, and its stderr names why.

id: harness-exec-text-file-busy
signature: Text file busy
class: harness exec of a written binary
first-seen: 2026-09-25
disposition: Opened 2026-09-27 off the hosted `build and test` job (run 36163173579, PR #204 at 75da1c2c), where `an_installed_binary_runs_from_the_sandbox_rather_than_its_source` could not exec its installed copy. `Sandbox::install_binary` wrote the copy through a descriptor of the test process, and a child forked by any other thread while that writer was open kept it past the close until the child's own exec, so the kernel refused the exec with ETXTBSY. Ruled 2026-09-28: the copy is written by a `cp` child, so the test process holds no writer to a file it executes. Two cases hold the copy in progress through a pipe, so each reads the same moment every run: `the_installing_process_holds_no_writer_to_a_binary_in_the_middle_of_its_copy` finds no writable descriptor of the test process to the installed file there, and `an_installed_binary_runs_while_a_child_forked_in_the_middle_of_its_copy_is_held_short_of_exec` forks a child there and runs the copy while that child is held, on Linux. The signature is the error text because the class spans every suite that installs a binary. A recurrence is a defect rather than a flake: a file written by the test process and then executed outside `install_binary`, or a regression in it.

id: heal-root-loss-backend-classification
signature: a_heal_refused_by_a_moved_root_leaves_a_subscription_that_drops
class: confirmed root loss hidden by a backend error
first-seen: 2026-10-01
disposition: Seen repeatedly in the macOS filesystem lane, first on PR #265 run36888968258: FSEvents reported the moved root as a Backend ENOENT before the anchor confirmed its identity changed. Fixed 2026-10-16: an independently confirmed CoverageLost supersedes a Backend symptom in the heal, synchronization and delivery paths, including a Backend already queued before that confirmation. Restoring the root does not demote the confirmed terminal cause. An unchanged root preserves its Backend error, and unrelated terminal causes stand. A recurrence is a product classification defect, not runner starvation, and requires diagnosis rather than a clearing rerun.

id: lifecycle-ready-before-the-leg-ends
signature: a_tick_passes_over_an_entry_a_marker_holds
class: harness reading a published state as the leg's end
first-seen: 2026-10-01
disposition: Opened 2026-10-01 off the hosted `build and test` job (run 36845533287, step 'Induced-failure recovery'), where the case's no-leg assertion found the attach leg still standing beside the marker it planted, and the poisoned gate buried the cause under follow-on panics. A job leg publishes `Ready` before it ends, so a case that reads `Ready` as no leg races that leg's teardown. Fixed 2026-10-16: the case waits for the entry's claim and leg to end before it schedules its marker, and the lifecycle cases that stand work on an entry, take its job channel, drive a tick or reap, or hand back its coverage right after a published state now wait for the leg that published it to end. A forced delay between a leg's work and its end, swept across the lifecycle suite, fails none of them. A recurrence is a defect rather than a flake.

id: lifecycle-reload-stranded-behind-the-attach-leg
signature: vault_reload_answers_a_stopped_host_with_no_code
class: harness reading a published state as the leg's end
first-seen: 2026-09-29
disposition: Opened 2026-09-29 off local stress runs (3 hangs in 1000 on main), where the case took the job channel after `Ready` while the attach leg still stood, so the reload it admitted was left to that leg's end and its reply was never sent. Fixed 2026-10-16, with lifecycle-ready-before-the-leg-ends: the case waits for the leg to end before it takes the channel. A recurrence is a defect rather than a flake.

id: lifecycle-reap-outruns-a-dropped-lease
signature: a_reap_that_unwinds_leaves_the_dispatcher_reaping_on_later_ticks
class: harness reading a published state as the leg's end
first-seen: 2026-09-29
disposition: Opened 2026-09-29 off a local run under CPU load, and seen again 2026-10-14 in a local workspace run: the case dropped its lease at once, so a slow attach left `Ready` standing only until the next reap tick, shorter than the wait's polling, and the wait breached its work bound reading `Unattached`. Fixed 2026-10-16: the lease stands until `Ready` is seen. A recurrence is a defect rather than a flake.
